/* Pharmacology 4–5: Hormones, muscle relaxants, local anaesthetics */
Z("pharm4",0,"ph",["Antithyroid drugs reduce thyroid hormone in hyperthyroidism: thioamides (carbimazole, methimazole, propylthiouracil) block hormone synthesis; iodides and radioactive iodine also reduce function; β-blockers control symptoms.","Thioamides jam the thyroid's assembly line (TPO enzyme); radioactive iodine is a 'Trojan horse' taken up by the gland that destroys it from inside.",["Thioamides: inhibit thyroid peroxidase (iodination and coupling); PTU also inhibits peripheral T4 → T3 conversion.","PTU vs carbimazole: PTU — first trimester pregnancy, thyroid storm, more hepatotoxic, shorter t½ (TDS); carbimazole (→ methimazole) — once daily, preferred generally, teratogenic (aplasia cutis) in 1st trimester.","ADR: rash, agranulocytosis (warn about sore throat/fever), hepatotoxicity (PTU), vasculitis.","Radioactive iodine (¹³¹I): Graves', toxic nodule, older patients; ADR — hypothyroidism; contraindicated in pregnancy, lactation. Thyroid storm: propranolol, PTU, Lugol's iodine (1 h after PTU), hydrocortisone, cooling, supportive."],"Thyroid storm: 'Block (β-blocker), Stop synthesis (PTU), Stop release (iodine), Steroids'."],
"Antithyroid drugs reduce thyroid hormone production or effects. Classification: (1) inhibitors of hormone synthesis (thioamides/thionamides) — propylthiouracil, methimazole, carbimazole; (2) inhibitors of iodide trapping (anion inhibitors) — perchlorate, thiocyanate, pertechnetate; (3) inhibitors of hormone release — iodine and iodides (Lugol's iodine, potassium iodide); (4) destruction of thyroid tissue — radioactive iodine (¹³¹I); (5) adjuncts — β-blockers (propranolol), corticosteroids, lithium, iodinated contrast (ipodate). Mechanism of thioamides: inhibit thyroid peroxidase, preventing oxidation of iodide, iodination of tyrosine residues in thyroglobulin and coupling of iodotyrosines to form T3 and T4; they do not affect release of stored hormone, so effect appears after 1–3 weeks (euthyroid in 4–8 weeks); PTU additionally inhibits peripheral deiodination of T4 to T3; thioamides may have immunosuppressive effects (reduce TRAb). Carbimazole is a prodrug converted to methimazole, which is 10 times more potent, longer acting (single daily dose), and accumulates in thyroid. Comparison: PTU — less potent, short t½ (1–2 h, given 6–8 hourly), highly protein bound (crosses placenta and enters milk less), inhibits peripheral conversion, preferred in first trimester of pregnancy and thyroid storm, but carries risk of severe hepatotoxicity (boxed warning) and ANCA vasculitis; carbimazole/methimazole — preferred for most patients (once daily, better compliance, less hepatotoxic), but associated with embryopathy (aplasia cutis, choanal/oesophageal atresia) in first trimester. Uses: Graves' disease (12–18 month course for remission), toxic nodular goitre (preparation), pre-operative preparation, adjunct to radioiodine, thyroid storm. Adverse effects: rashes, urticaria, arthralgia (commonest), GI upset, loss of taste, agranulocytosis (0.2–0.5%; usually in first 3 months — patients warned to stop drug and report fever or sore throat), aplastic anaemia, hepatotoxicity (PTU — hepatocellular; methimazole — cholestatic), lupus-like syndrome, vasculitis, hypothyroidism and goitre on overdose. Radioactive iodine (¹³¹I; t½ 8 days; β and γ emitter): concentrated by thyroid, β particles destroy follicular cells over 2–3 months; given orally; uses — Graves' disease (especially > 40 years, relapses), toxic multinodular goitre and toxic adenoma, thyroid carcinoma (ablation, metastases); advantages — simple, cheap, permanent; disadvantages — delayed effect, hypothyroidism (most patients eventually), worsening of ophthalmopathy (give steroid cover), contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation, avoid conception for 6 months. Iodides: high doses (Wolff–Chaikoff effect) rapidly inhibit hormone release and synthesis; reduce gland vascularity before thyroidectomy (7–10 days), thyroid storm (given 1 hour after thioamide), radiation emergency (KI); escape occurs in 2–3 weeks. Thyroid storm: life-threatening exacerbation (fever, tachyarrhythmias, heart failure, agitation, delirium, vomiting, diarrhoea) precipitated by infection, surgery, radioiodine; treatment — propranolol (IV/oral; also blocks T4 → T3 conversion) or esmolol, PTU 200–300 mg 6-hourly (or methimazole), Lugol's iodine/potassium iodide (1 h after thioamide), IV hydrocortisone 100 mg 8-hourly (reduces conversion, treats relative adrenal insufficiency), cooling with paracetamol (avoid aspirin — displaces T4), IV fluids, treatment of precipitating cause, plasmapheresis in refractory cases.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs","Mechanism"],["Thioamides","Carbimazole, methimazole, PTU","Inhibit TPO (synthesis)"],["Anion inhibitors","Perchlorate, thiocyanate","Block iodide uptake (NIS)"],["Iodides","Lugol's iodine, KI","Inhibit release (Wolff–Chaikoff)"],["Radioactive iodine","¹³¹I","Destroys thyroid tissue"],["Adjuncts","Propranolol, steroids","Symptom control, ↓ T4→T3"]]],
["PTU vs carbimazole",[["Feature","PTU","Carbimazole/methimazole"],["Potency","Lower","10× higher"],["t½ / dosing","Short; 3 times daily","Long; once daily"],["Peripheral T4→T3 block","Yes","No"],["Pregnancy","1st trimester","2nd/3rd trimester"],["Hepatotoxicity","Severe (hepatocellular)","Less (cholestatic)"],["Thyroid storm","Preferred","Alternative"]]],
["Thyroid storm management",["Propranolol / esmolol","PTU (or methimazole)","Lugol's iodine 1 h after PTU","IV hydrocortisone","Cooling, fluids, treat precipitant"]]],
{c:"Thioamides block hormone synthesis and are first-line for Graves' disease, with radioiodine for definitive therapy; thyroid storm needs combined blockade of synthesis, release, peripheral conversion and adrenergic effects."});

Z("pharm4",1,"ph",["Oral hypoglycaemic drugs lower blood sugar in type 2 diabetes by different mechanisms: increasing insulin release (sulfonylureas), reducing liver glucose output and improving insulin sensitivity (metformin), or other routes (DPP-4 inhibitors, SGLT2 inhibitors, glitazones).","Sulfonylureas press the pancreas's insulin-release button; metformin turns down the liver's sugar tap and helps muscles use sugar.",["Classification: sulfonylureas (glibenclamide, gliclazide, glimepiride, glipizide), meglitinides (repaglinide), biguanides (metformin), thiazolidinediones (pioglitazone), α-glucosidase inhibitors (acarbose, voglibose), DPP-4 inhibitors (sitagliptin, vildagliptin, teneligliptin), SGLT2 inhibitors (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin), oral GLP-1 agonist (semaglutide).","Sulfonylureas: block K⁺-ATP channels on β cells → depolarisation → Ca²⁺ entry → insulin release; ADR — hypoglycaemia (glibenclamide), weight gain, disulfiram reaction (chlorpropamide), SIADH.","Metformin: activates AMPK → ↓ hepatic gluconeogenesis, ↑ peripheral glucose uptake, ↓ intestinal absorption; no hypoglycaemia, weight neutral/loss, first-line; also PCOS, prediabetes.","Metformin ADR: GI upset (commonest), metallic taste, B12 deficiency, lactic acidosis (rare; avoid eGFR < 30, hypoxia, contrast, alcohol, liver failure)."],"Metformin = first-line: 'no hypo, no weight gain, B12 & lactic acidosis caution'."],
"Oral antidiabetic drugs are used in type 2 diabetes mellitus. Classification: (A) insulin secretagogues — sulfonylureas: first-generation (tolbutamide, chlorpropamide — obsolete), second-generation (glibenclamide/glyburide, glipizide, gliclazide, glimepiride); meglitinide/phenylalanine analogues (repaglinide, nateglinide); (B) biguanides — metformin; (C) thiazolidinediones (PPAR-γ agonists) — pioglitazone (rosiglitazone restricted); (D) α-glucosidase inhibitors — acarbose, miglitol, voglibose; (E) dipeptidyl peptidase-4 inhibitors (gliptins) — sitagliptin, vildagliptin, saxagliptin, linagliptin, teneligliptin; (F) sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 inhibitors (gliflozins) — dapagliflozin, empagliflozin, canagliflozin, remogliflozin; (G) others — oral semaglutide (GLP-1 agonist), bromocriptine-QR, colesevelam, dual PPAR agonist saroglitazar (India). Sulfonylureas: mechanism — bind SUR1 subunit of ATP-sensitive K⁺ channels on pancreatic β cells, closing them, causing depolarisation, opening of voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels and insulin exocytosis; require functioning β cells; extrapancreatic effects (reduced hepatic insulin clearance) minor; pharmacokinetics — well absorbed, highly protein bound, metabolised in liver, renal excretion (glibenclamide active metabolites accumulate in renal failure); uses — type 2 DM not controlled by metformin and lifestyle, as add-on; adverse effects — hypoglycaemia (most important; prolonged with glibenclamide and chlorpropamide in elderly and renal impairment; gliclazide and glimepiride safer), weight gain, GI upset, rashes, hypersensitivity, haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, cholestatic jaundice, disulfiram-like reaction and SIADH (chlorpropamide), possible cardiovascular concerns; interactions — drugs displacing from protein binding (sulfonamides, NSAIDs) or inhibiting metabolism (fluconazole) cause hypoglycaemia; β-blockers mask hypoglycaemic warning. Metformin: mechanism — activates AMP-activated protein kinase (and inhibits mitochondrial glycerophosphate dehydrogenase/complex I), suppressing hepatic gluconeogenesis (main effect), increasing insulin-mediated glucose uptake and utilisation in skeletal muscle, reducing intestinal glucose absorption, increasing GLP-1; does not stimulate insulin release — hence no hypoglycaemia in monotherapy; reduces weight or is weight neutral; lowers LDL and triglycerides; reduces cardiovascular events (UKPDS); pharmacokinetics — not metabolised, excreted unchanged by kidney, t½ 3 h (extended-release available); uses — first-line drug for type 2 DM (with lifestyle), especially obese; prediabetes (prevention), PCOS (insulin resistance, ovulation), gestational diabetes (selected), antipsychotic-induced weight gain; dose 500 mg–2 g/day with meals, titrated; adverse effects — GI (anorexia, nausea, abdominal discomfort, metallic taste, diarrhoea — commonest, minimised by gradual titration and XR), vitamin B12 deficiency with long-term use (check levels), lactic acidosis (rare but serious; risk in renal impairment — avoid if eGFR < 30, reduce dose if 30–45; hepatic failure, alcoholism, heart failure, hypoxia, sepsis, shock; withhold before iodinated contrast and major surgery). Other classes: pioglitazone — PPAR-γ agonist, insulin sensitiser; ADR weight gain, fluid retention, heart failure, fractures, bladder cancer concern; DPP-4 inhibitors — enhance incretins, weight neutral, low hypoglycaemia; SGLT2 inhibitors — glycosuria, weight loss, BP lowering, cardiorenal protection; ADR genital mycotic infections, euglycaemic DKA, volume depletion; α-glucosidase inhibitors — reduce postprandial glucose; flatulence.",
[["Classification and mechanisms",[["Class","Drugs","Mechanism"],["Sulfonylureas","Glibenclamide, gliclazide, glimepiride","Close K-ATP → insulin release"],["Meglitinides","Repaglinide","Short-acting secretagogue"],["Biguanides","Metformin","↓ Hepatic glucose output (AMPK)"],["Thiazolidinediones","Pioglitazone","PPAR-γ → insulin sensitivity"],["α-Glucosidase inhibitors","Acarbose, voglibose","↓ Carbohydrate absorption"],["DPP-4 inhibitors","Sitagliptin, vildagliptin, teneligliptin","↑ GLP-1, GIP"],["SGLT2 inhibitors","Dapagliflozin, empagliflozin","Glycosuria"]]],
["Sulfonylureas vs metformin",[["Feature","Sulfonylureas","Metformin"],["Mechanism","↑ Insulin secretion","↓ Hepatic gluconeogenesis, ↑ sensitivity"],["Needs β cells","Yes","No"],["Hypoglycaemia","Yes","No (alone)"],["Weight","Gain","Loss/neutral"],["Lipids","No effect","Improve"],["Major ADR","Hypoglycaemia","GI upset, B12 deficiency, lactic acidosis"],["Renal failure","Avoid long-acting","Avoid if eGFR < 30"]]]],
{c:"Metformin is the first-line oral drug in type 2 diabetes; sulfonylureas and newer classes are added according to glycaemic targets, weight, hypoglycaemia risk and cardiorenal comorbidity."});

Z("pharm4",2,"ph",["Type 2 diabetes is managed with lifestyle changes plus drugs, starting with metformin and adding other agents (SGLT2 inhibitors, GLP-1 agonists, DPP-4 inhibitors, sulfonylureas, insulin) based on targets and comorbidities, while controlling BP, lipids and screening for complications.","Treat the whole person, not just the sugar: diet and exercise first, metformin as foundation, then choose add-ons that also protect heart and kidneys.",["Targets: HbA1c < 7% (individualise), FPG 80–130 mg/dL, PPG < 180; BP < 130/80; LDL < 100 (< 70 high risk).","Lifestyle: diet (calorie, carbohydrate quality), 150 min/week exercise, weight loss, stop smoking.","Drugs: metformin first; with ASCVD, HF or CKD — SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 RA regardless of HbA1c; then DPP-4i, sulfonylurea, pioglitazone; insulin when HbA1c very high (> 10%), symptomatic, pregnancy, failure of orals.","Also: statin, ACE inhibitor/ARB (albuminuria), aspirin (secondary prevention), annual eye, foot, kidney checks, vaccinations."],"T2DM: 'Lifestyle → Metformin → Add (SGLT2/GLP-1 if heart/kidney) → Insulin'."],
"Management of type 2 diabetes aims to relieve symptoms, achieve glycaemic control, prevent acute and chronic complications and improve quality of life. Targets (ADA/RSSDI): HbA1c < 7% for most adults (< 6.5% if achievable without hypoglycaemia in young; < 8% in elderly with comorbidities), fasting/preprandial glucose 80–130 mg/dL, postprandial < 180 mg/dL, BP < 130/80 mmHg, LDL < 100 mg/dL (< 70 or < 55 in ASCVD), triglycerides < 150 mg/dL, BMI and waist reduction. Components: (1) patient education and self-management, self-monitoring of blood glucose; (2) medical nutrition therapy — individualised calorie intake, complex carbohydrates with low glycaemic index, high fibre, limit saturated fats and sugars, regular meal timing; (3) physical activity — ≥ 150 minutes/week moderate aerobic plus resistance exercise; weight loss of 5–10% (or more for remission); smoking cessation, alcohol moderation; (4) pharmacotherapy — metformin is first-line (unless contraindicated); in patients with established atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease, heart failure or chronic kidney disease, an SGLT2 inhibitor (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin) and/or GLP-1 receptor agonist (liraglutide, semaglutide, dulaglutide) with proven cardiorenal benefit is recommended irrespective of HbA1c; for weight management, GLP-1 RA or tirzepatide (GIP/GLP-1 agonist); if HbA1c above target after 3 months, add a second agent — sulfonylurea (low cost; gliclazide, glimepiride), DPP-4 inhibitor (low hypoglycaemia, weight neutral), pioglitazone, SGLT2 inhibitor, GLP-1 RA; triple therapy if needed; insulin — basal insulin (glargine, degludec, NPH at bedtime) added to orals when HbA1c remains high, intensified to basal–bolus or premixed as needed; early insulin if HbA1c > 10% or glucose ≥ 300 mg/dL with symptoms (catabolic), DKA/HHS, pregnancy, perioperative, severe infections, hepatic or renal failure where orals are unsuitable; (5) management of comorbidities — antihypertensives (ACE inhibitor or ARB preferred with albuminuria), statins for most patients aged ≥ 40, aspirin for secondary prevention; (6) screening and prevention of complications — annual dilated fundus examination, urine albumin–creatinine ratio and eGFR, foot examination (monofilament, pulses), lipid profile, ECG, dental care; vaccinations (influenza, pneumococcal, hepatitis B); (7) management of hypoglycaemia (15–20 g glucose; glucagon) and sick-day rules.",
[["Stepwise pharmacotherapy",[["Step","Approach"],["1","Lifestyle + metformin"],["With ASCVD/HF/CKD","Add SGLT2i and/or GLP-1 RA"],["2","Add DPP-4i, SU, pioglitazone, SGLT2i or GLP-1 RA"],["3","Triple therapy or basal insulin"],["4","Basal–bolus/premixed insulin"]]],
["Targets",[["Parameter","Target"],["HbA1c","< 7% (individualised)"],["Fasting glucose","80–130 mg/dL"],["Postprandial glucose","< 180 mg/dL"],["BP","< 130/80 mmHg"],["LDL","< 100 (< 70 high risk) mg/dL"]]]],
{c:"Type 2 diabetes care is multifactorial: lifestyle, metformin-based stepwise therapy with cardiorenal-protective agents where indicated, insulin when needed, and control of cardiovascular risk factors."});

Z("pharm4",3,"ph",["Corticosteroids are hormones of the adrenal cortex (glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids); synthetic glucocorticoids are powerful anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive drugs with many uses and many side effects.","Steroids are a strong fire hose for inflammation and immune overactivity — they put out many fires, but long use floods the house (side effects).",["Classification: short-acting (hydrocortisone, cortisone), intermediate (prednisolone, methylprednisolone, triamcinolone), long-acting (dexamethasone, betamethasone); mineralocorticoid — fludrocortisone; inhaled — budesonide, fluticasone.","MOA: enter cell → bind cytoplasmic GR → nucleus → ↑ lipocortin (annexin-1, inhibits PLA2), ↓ COX-2, cytokines (IL-1, IL-2, TNF), NF-κB; ↓ lymphocytes, eosinophils.","Uses: replacement (Addison's, CAH), inflammatory/autoimmune (RA, SLE, asthma, IBD, nephrotic syndrome), allergy/anaphylaxis, transplant, malignancy (ALL, lymphoma), cerebral oedema, fetal lung maturity, shock, skin, eye.","ADR (Cushingoid): moon face, buffalo hump, hyperglycaemia, hypertension, osteoporosis, myopathy, infections, peptic ulcer, cataract/glaucoma, psychosis, growth retardation, HPA suppression → adrenal crisis on abrupt withdrawal."],"Steroid ADR 'CUSHINGOID': Cataract, Ulcer, Skin thin, Hypertension/Hyperglycaemia, Infection, Necrosis (AVN), Growth ↓, Osteoporosis, Immunosuppression, Diabetes."],
"Corticosteroids are adrenocortical steroid hormones — glucocorticoids (cortisol; metabolic and anti-inflammatory effects) and mineralocorticoids (aldosterone; salt and water retention) — and their synthetic analogues. Classification: glucocorticoids — short-acting (biological t½ 8–12 h): hydrocortisone (cortisol; significant mineralocorticoid activity), cortisone; intermediate-acting (12–36 h): prednisolone, prednisone, methylprednisolone, triamcinolone, deflazacort; long-acting (36–72 h): dexamethasone, betamethasone (25–30 times more potent than hydrocortisone, no mineralocorticoid activity); mineralocorticoids — fludrocortisone, desoxycorticosterone; inhaled (budesonide, beclomethasone, fluticasone, ciclesonide); topical (clobetasol, mometasone, hydrocortisone); intranasal and ophthalmic preparations. Mechanism: lipophilic glucocorticoids enter cells and bind cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptors (complexed with heat-shock proteins); the activated complex translocates to the nucleus and binds glucocorticoid response elements, inducing anti-inflammatory proteins (lipocortin/annexin-1 inhibiting phospholipase A2 and thereby prostaglandin and leukotriene synthesis; IκB; MAP kinase phosphatase-1) and repressing pro-inflammatory genes by interfering with transcription factors NF-κB and AP-1 (transrepression) — reduced synthesis of cytokines (IL-1, IL-2, IL-6, TNF-α, IFN-γ), chemokines, COX-2, iNOS, adhesion molecules; result — reduced capillary permeability and leucocyte migration, lymphocytopenia (redistribution and apoptosis), eosinopenia, monocytopenia, neutrophilia, decreased fibroblast activity, inhibition of antibody production at high doses. Metabolic actions: gluconeogenesis, hyperglycaemia, protein catabolism, lipolysis and fat redistribution, negative calcium balance; mineralocorticoid actions; CNS, CVS (permissive for catecholamines), stomach, blood. Uses: (A) replacement therapy — acute adrenal insufficiency (IV hydrocortisone), chronic adrenal insufficiency/Addison disease (hydrocortisone + fludrocortisone), congenital adrenal hyperplasia (suppress ACTH); (B) pharmacotherapeutic (non-endocrine) — arthritis (RA — bridging, intra-articular; acute gout), collagen diseases (SLE, polymyositis, vasculitis), severe allergic reactions (anaphylaxis after adrenaline, angioedema, serum sickness, drug reactions), bronchial asthma and COPD exacerbations, renal disease (minimal change nephrotic syndrome), haematological (AIHA, ITP, ALL, lymphomas, myeloma), autoimmune hepatitis, IBD, skin diseases (pemphigus, eczema, psoriasis — topical), eye diseases (uveitis, allergic conjunctivitis — not in herpetic keratitis), organ transplantation, cerebral oedema due to tumours (dexamethasone), spinal cord injury (methylprednisolone — controversial), septic shock (hydrocortisone in refractory), tuberculous meningitis/pericarditis, Pneumocystis pneumonia with hypoxia, severe COVID-19 (dexamethasone), antenatal for fetal lung maturity (betamethasone/dexamethasone before 34 weeks), chemotherapy-induced vomiting (dexamethasone), hypercalcaemia (sarcoid, myeloma), thyroid storm, Bell palsy, leprosy reactions, raised ICP in cerebral malaria (not used). Adverse effects (mostly with prolonged systemic use): Cushingoid habitus (moon face, buffalo hump, truncal obesity, striae), hyperglycaemia and diabetes, hypertension, sodium and water retention, hypokalaemia (mineralocorticoid), muscle wasting and myopathy, osteoporosis and fractures, avascular necrosis of femoral head, growth retardation in children, peptic ulceration (especially with NSAIDs), susceptibility to infections and reactivation of TB, masking of infection signs, delayed wound healing, thinning of skin, acne, hirsutism, cataract (posterior subcapsular) and glaucoma, psychiatric disturbances (euphoria, insomnia, psychosis, depression), pancreatitis, HPA axis suppression — abrupt withdrawal after > 2–3 weeks can precipitate acute adrenal crisis, withdrawal syndrome or flare of disease (taper gradually; alternate-day therapy reduces suppression); steroid cover needed during surgery and stress. Contraindications/caution: peptic ulcer, diabetes, hypertension, osteoporosis, active infections (TB, herpes simplex keratitis, fungal), psychosis, epilepsy, pregnancy (use with care), CHF, glaucoma, renal failure. Precautions: lowest effective dose for shortest time, morning dosing, local routes where possible, calcium + vitamin D ± bisphosphonate, screen for TB/diabetes, steroid card.",
[["Classification",[["Drug","Anti-inflammatory potency","Mineralocorticoid","Duration"],["Hydrocortisone","1","++","Short"],["Prednisolone","4","+","Intermediate"],["Methylprednisolone","5","±","Intermediate"],["Triamcinolone","5","0","Intermediate"],["Dexamethasone","25–30","0","Long"],["Betamethasone","25–30","0","Long"],["Fludrocortisone","10","+++++","—"]]],
["Mechanism",["Bind cytoplasmic GR → nucleus → GRE","↑ Lipocortin (annexin-1) → ↓ PLA2 → ↓ PGs, LTs","↓ NF-κB, AP-1 → ↓ cytokines (IL-1, IL-2, TNF), COX-2","↓ Lymphocytes, eosinophils; ↓ capillary permeability"]],
["Uses",["Replacement: Addison, CAH","Inflammatory/autoimmune: RA, SLE, vasculitis, IBD","Asthma, COPD exacerbations","Allergies, anaphylaxis (adjunct)","Nephrotic syndrome","ALL, lymphoma, myeloma","Transplant rejection","Cerebral oedema (dexamethasone)","Fetal lung maturity","Septic shock (refractory)"]],
["Adverse effects",["Cushingoid features","Hyperglycaemia, hypertension, oedema, hypokalaemia","Osteoporosis, avascular necrosis, myopathy","Infections, TB reactivation","Peptic ulcer","Cataract, glaucoma","Psychosis","Growth retardation","HPA suppression — adrenal crisis on abrupt withdrawal"]]],
{i:"Glucocorticoids are among the most powerful anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive drugs; their benefits must be balanced against dose- and duration-dependent toxicity.",x:[["Contraindications",["Peptic ulcer, diabetes, hypertension","Osteoporosis, glaucoma","Active TB, herpes simplex keratitis, systemic fungal infection","Psychosis, epilepsy","CHF, pregnancy (relative)"]],["Withdrawal",["Taper after > 2–3 weeks of therapy","Alternate-day dosing reduces HPA suppression","Steroid cover during stress/surgery"]]],f:"Glucocorticoid → cytoplasmic GR → nucleus\n ├ transactivation: ↑ lipocortin → ↓ PLA2 → ↓ PGs/LTs\n └ transrepression: ↓ NF-κB/AP-1 → ↓ cytokines, COX-2\n → anti-inflammatory, immunosuppressive\n Long-term: Cushingoid ADRs, HPA suppression",c:"Corticosteroids have wide therapeutic uses; using the lowest effective dose, preferring local routes, monitoring for adverse effects and tapering slowly ensure safe therapy."});

Z("pharm4",4,"ph",["Diabetic ketoacidosis is a dangerous complication of insulin deficiency with high blood sugar, ketones and acidosis; treatment is IV fluids, IV insulin, potassium replacement and treating the trigger.","Without insulin, cells starve despite sugar in the blood, so the body burns fat and makes acids (ketones); fluids, insulin and potassium reverse the crisis.",["Diagnosis: glucose > 250 mg/dL, pH < 7.3, HCO3 < 18, ketonaemia/ketonuria, anion gap.","Fluids: 0.9% NaCl 1 L in first hour, then 250–500 mL/h; add 5% dextrose when glucose < 250 mg/dL.","Insulin: regular insulin IV infusion 0.1 U/kg/h (± 0.1 U/kg bolus) after K⁺ ≥ 3.3 mmol/L; continue till ketosis resolves; overlap SC insulin 1–2 h before stopping IV.","Potassium: if K⁺ < 3.3 hold insulin, replace; 3.3–5.3 add 20–40 mEq/L; bicarbonate only if pH < 6.9; monitor glucose hourly, electrolytes 2–4 hourly; watch cerebral oedema (children), hypoglycaemia, hypokalaemia."],"DKA treatment order: 'Fluids first, then Insulin, always Potassium'."],
"Diabetic ketoacidosis is an acute metabolic complication, usually of type 1 diabetes (also type 2 under stress, SGLT2 inhibitors — euglycaemic DKA), characterised by hyperglycaemia (> 250 mg/dL), metabolic acidosis (pH < 7.3, bicarbonate < 18 mEq/L, raised anion gap) and ketonaemia/ketonuria. Precipitants: infections, missed insulin doses, new-onset diabetes, MI, stroke, pancreatitis, trauma, drugs (steroids, SGLT2i). Pathogenesis: absolute or relative insulin deficiency with excess counter-regulatory hormones (glucagon, catecholamines, cortisol, GH) → increased hepatic gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, reduced peripheral glucose utilisation → hyperglycaemia → osmotic diuresis → dehydration and electrolyte loss; increased lipolysis → free fatty acids → hepatic β-oxidation → ketone bodies (acetoacetate, β-hydroxybutyrate) → metabolic acidosis. Clinical: polyuria, polydipsia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, Kussmaul breathing, fruity (acetone) breath, dehydration, hypotension, altered sensorium. Management principles: (1) fluid replacement — most important initial step; isotonic saline 15–20 mL/kg (1–1.5 L) in the first hour, then 250–500 mL/h depending on hydration and corrected sodium (switch to 0.45% saline if corrected Na normal/high); add 5% dextrose when glucose falls to 200–250 mg/dL to allow continued insulin until ketosis clears; replace about 6–9 L deficit over 24–48 h; (2) insulin — regular (soluble) insulin by continuous IV infusion 0.1 U/kg/h (optional 0.1 U/kg IV bolus) or 0.14 U/kg/h without bolus, started after confirming K⁺ ≥ 3.3 mmol/L; aim fall of glucose by 50–75 mg/dL/h; continue until acidosis and ketonaemia resolve (pH > 7.3, HCO3 ≥ 15–18, anion gap normal), then start subcutaneous basal–bolus insulin with 1–2 h overlap before stopping infusion; (3) potassium — total body depletion despite initial normal/high serum K⁺ (acidosis and insulin deficiency shift K⁺ out); insulin and correction of acidosis lower K⁺; if K⁺ < 3.3, give 20–30 mEq/h KCl and withhold insulin; if 3.3–5.2, add 20–30 mEq to each litre to keep 4–5; if > 5.2, do not add but recheck 2-hourly; (4) bicarbonate — only if pH < 6.9 (risk of hypokalaemia, paradoxical CNS acidosis, cerebral oedema); (5) phosphate if < 1 mg/dL; magnesium if low; (6) treat precipitating cause (antibiotics for infection), thromboprophylaxis; (7) monitoring — glucose hourly, electrolytes, venous pH and ketones 2–4 hourly, urine output, ECG, GCS. Complications of therapy: hypoglycaemia, hypokalaemia, hyperchloraemic acidosis, cerebral oedema (especially children — avoid rapid fluid and osmolality shifts), ARDS, thromboembolism.",
[["Diagnostic criteria",[["Parameter","DKA"],["Glucose","> 250 mg/dL"],["Arterial pH","< 7.3"],["Bicarbonate","< 18 mEq/L"],["Ketones","Positive (serum/urine)"],["Anion gap","> 12"]]],
["Management",[["Component","Action"],["Fluids","0.9% NaCl 1 L first hour, then 250–500 mL/h; dextrose when glucose < 250"],["Insulin","Regular insulin IV 0.1 U/kg/h after K⁺ ≥ 3.3"],["Potassium","Replace to keep 4–5 mmol/L"],["Bicarbonate","Only if pH < 6.9"],["Cause","Treat infection/precipitant"],["Transition","SC insulin overlap 1–2 h"]]]],
{c:"DKA is reversed by aggressive fluid therapy, IV insulin and meticulous potassium replacement, with close monitoring and treatment of the precipitating cause."});

Z("pharm4",5,"ph",["5α-reductase inhibitors (finasteride, dutasteride) block the conversion of testosterone to the more potent dihydrotestosterone (DHT), shrinking an enlarged prostate and reducing male-pattern hair loss.","DHT is the fertiliser for prostate growth and scalp hair follicle shrinkage; blocking its production shrinks the prostate over months.",["Finasteride: type 2 5α-reductase inhibitor; dutasteride: types 1 and 2.","Uses: BPH (5 mg; reduces prostate size by ~25%, urinary retention, need for surgery — effect in 3–6 months; combine with α-blocker), male pattern baldness (finasteride 1 mg), hirsutism.","↓ Serum PSA by ~50% — double the value when interpreting.","ADR: decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, ejaculatory disorders, gynaecomastia; teratogenic — women of childbearing age should not handle crushed tablets (feminisation of male fetus)."],"Finasteride: 'Finishes DHT — shrinks prostate, halves PSA'."],
"5α-reductase converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT), the potent androgen responsible for prostate growth, male external genitalia development, androgenetic alopecia and acne. Type 2 isoenzyme predominates in prostate, seminal vesicles, hair follicles; type 1 in skin and liver. Finasteride is a competitive inhibitor of type 2 5α-reductase; dutasteride inhibits both types 1 and 2, producing greater suppression of DHT (> 90%) and longer t½ (5 weeks). Effects: decrease serum and intraprostatic DHT, reduce prostate volume by 20–30% over 6 months, improve urinary flow and symptoms, reduce risk of acute urinary retention and need for prostate surgery; slower onset than α-blockers; most effective in large glands (> 40 mL); do not lower testosterone (may slightly increase). Uses: benign prostatic hyperplasia (finasteride 5 mg or dutasteride 0.5 mg daily; combination with tamsulosin for large prostates — MTOPS, CombAT trials), male pattern baldness (finasteride 1 mg daily; topical formulations), hirsutism in women (off-label, with contraception), reduction of haematuria from BPH. Effect on PSA: lowers serum PSA by about 50% after 6 months — PSA values should be doubled for cancer screening; a rise while on therapy warrants evaluation. Adverse effects: decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, reduced ejaculate volume, gynaecomastia and breast tenderness, depression; persistent sexual side effects reported (post-finasteride syndrome); possible increased detection of high-grade prostate cancer. Contraindications: women who are or may become pregnant (risk of hypospadias and ambiguous genitalia in male fetus — avoid handling crushed tablets; men on dutasteride should not donate blood for 6 months), children.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Finasteride","Dutasteride"],["Isoenzymes inhibited","Type 2","Types 1 and 2"],["DHT suppression","~70%","> 90%"],["t½","6–8 h","~5 weeks"],["Dose (BPH)","5 mg/day","0.5 mg/day"],["Baldness","1 mg/day","Off-label"]]]],
{c:"5α-reductase inhibitors provide slow but durable relief in BPH and treat androgenetic alopecia; PSA must be adjusted and pregnant women must avoid exposure."});

Z("pharm4",6,"ph",["Sildenafil is a phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor that enhances the nitric oxide–cGMP pathway, improving penile erection with sexual stimulation; it is also used for pulmonary arterial hypertension.","Sexual stimulation releases NO, which makes cGMP to relax penile vessels; sildenafil stops PDE5 from breaking cGMP down, so the relaxation lasts.",["MOA: inhibits PDE5 → ↑ cGMP → smooth muscle relaxation in corpus cavernosum (needs sexual stimulation).","Uses: erectile dysfunction (50 mg 1 h before), pulmonary arterial hypertension (20 mg TDS), Raynaud's (off-label), high-altitude pulmonary oedema.","ADR: headache, flushing, dyspepsia, nasal congestion, visual disturbance (blue tinge — PDE6), hypotension, priapism, NAION, sudden hearing loss.","Contraindicated with nitrates (severe hypotension) and riociguat; caution with α-blockers, CYP3A4 inhibitors; tadalafil (longer — 36 h), vardenafil, avanafil."],"Sildenafil + nitrates = dangerous hypotension."],
"Sildenafil is a selective inhibitor of phosphodiesterase type 5 (PDE5), the enzyme that degrades cyclic GMP in the corpus cavernosum and pulmonary vasculature. Mechanism: sexual stimulation releases nitric oxide from nerve endings and endothelium of penile vessels, activating guanylyl cyclase to produce cGMP, which relaxes cavernosal smooth muscle and arterioles, increasing blood inflow and producing erection; sildenafil prevents cGMP breakdown, enhancing and prolonging the erectile response; it has no effect without sexual stimulation and does not increase libido. Pharmacokinetics: oral bioavailability 40%, peak in 1 h (delayed by fatty meal), t½ 4 h, metabolised by CYP3A4 (levels increased by ketoconazole, erythromycin, ritonavir, grapefruit). Uses: erectile dysfunction of various causes (psychogenic, diabetic, spinal cord injury, post-prostatectomy, antidepressant-induced) — 25–100 mg taken about 1 h before sexual activity, not more than once daily; pulmonary arterial hypertension (20 mg three times daily; Revatio); off-label — Raynaud phenomenon, high-altitude pulmonary oedema prevention, fetal growth restriction (research), female sexual arousal disorder (not established). Adverse effects: headache, flushing, dyspepsia, nasal congestion, dizziness, back pain/myalgia (tadalafil), transient blue-green colour vision disturbance and photosensitivity (PDE6 inhibition in retina), hypotension, syncope, priapism (> 4 h — emergency), non-arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy, sudden sensorineural hearing loss, myocardial ischaemia in susceptible persons (due to exertion). Contraindications: concurrent use of organic nitrates or nitric oxide donors (nitroglycerin, isosorbide, amyl nitrite) — profound hypotension (wait 24 h; 48 h for tadalafil); riociguat; recent MI or stroke, unstable angina, severe hypotension, severe hepatic impairment, retinitis pigmentosa, conditions in which sexual activity is inadvisable; caution with α-blockers (start low), anatomical penile deformity, sickle cell disease. Other PDE5 inhibitors: tadalafil (long t½ 17.5 h, effective up to 36 h — 'weekend pill'; also daily for BPH), vardenafil, avanafil (rapid onset).",
[["PDE5 inhibitors",[["Drug","Onset","Duration","Notes"],["Sildenafil","1 h","4–6 h","Food delays; blue vision"],["Tadalafil","30 min–2 h","Up to 36 h","Daily for BPH; myalgia"],["Vardenafil","1 h","4–6 h","QT caution"],["Avanafil","15–30 min","6 h","Fast onset"]]]],
{c:"Sildenafil enhances NO–cGMP-mediated vasodilatation, effective in erectile dysfunction and pulmonary hypertension; co-administration with nitrates is absolutely contraindicated."});

Z("pharm4",7,"ph",["Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) gives oestrogen (with progestin if the uterus is intact) to relieve menopausal symptoms and prevent bone loss; it is used at the lowest dose for the shortest necessary time.","HRT replaces the falling oestrogen supply after menopause to calm hot flushes and protect bones, but long-term use carries risks of clots and breast cancer.",["Indications: vasomotor symptoms (hot flushes, night sweats), genitourinary syndrome (vaginal atrophy — local oestrogen), premature ovarian insufficiency, osteoporosis prevention when other drugs unsuitable.","Regimens: oestrogen alone (hysterectomised); oestrogen + progestin (cyclic or continuous) if uterus present — prevents endometrial cancer; tibolone; transdermal preferred (less VTE).","Risks (WHI): VTE, stroke, breast cancer (combined), endometrial cancer (unopposed oestrogen), gall bladder disease; benefits — ↓ fractures, ↓ colorectal cancer.","Contraindications: breast/endometrial cancer, undiagnosed vaginal bleeding, active VTE, liver disease, recent MI/stroke."],"HRT rule: 'Uterus present → add Progestin'."],
"Hormone replacement therapy (menopausal hormone therapy) is the administration of oestrogen, alone or with a progestin, to women with symptoms of oestrogen deficiency after natural or surgical menopause. Indications: moderate to severe vasomotor symptoms (hot flushes, night sweats, sleep disturbance) — most effective treatment; genitourinary syndrome of menopause (vaginal dryness, dyspareunia, recurrent UTI — low-dose vaginal oestrogen); prevention of postmenopausal osteoporosis and fractures in women at high risk when other agents are unsuitable; premature ovarian insufficiency and early surgical menopause (until the average age of menopause); mood and sleep symptoms related to menopause. Preparations: oestrogens — conjugated equine oestrogens, oestradiol valerate, 17β-oestradiol (oral, transdermal patch or gel, vaginal tablets/cream/ring), ethinyl oestradiol; progestins — medroxyprogesterone acetate, norethisterone, dydrogesterone, micronised progesterone, levonorgestrel IUS; tibolone (synthetic steroid with oestrogenic, progestogenic and androgenic activity); SERMs (raloxifene — bone only, may worsen flushes); ospemifene (vaginal atrophy); non-hormonal alternatives — SSRIs/SNRIs (paroxetine, venlafaxine), gabapentin, clonidine, fezolinetant (NK3 antagonist). Regimens: women without a uterus — oestrogen alone; women with a uterus — oestrogen plus progestin to prevent endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma: sequential/cyclic (progestin 10–14 days each month; withdrawal bleed) in perimenopause, continuous combined in postmenopause (no bleed after initial months). Transdermal route avoids first-pass hepatic effects and is associated with lower risk of VTE and stroke. Benefits: relief of symptoms, prevention of bone loss and fractures, reduced colorectal cancer, possibly reduced coronary disease if started early ('timing hypothesis' — within 10 years of menopause or before age 60). Risks (Women's Health Initiative and others): venous thromboembolism, stroke, breast cancer (with combined therapy, increasing with duration > 3–5 years), endometrial cancer with unopposed oestrogen, gall bladder disease, coronary events if started late, dementia in older women; side effects — breast tenderness, nausea, bloating, headache, irregular bleeding. Contraindications: known or suspected breast cancer or oestrogen-dependent cancer, undiagnosed abnormal genital bleeding, active or past VTE, active arterial thromboembolic disease (recent MI, stroke), active liver disease, pregnancy, porphyria. Principles: individualise; use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration consistent with treatment goals; annual review; mammography and breast examination; evaluate abnormal bleeding.",
[["Regimens",[["Situation","Regimen"],["Hysterectomised","Oestrogen alone"],["Uterus intact, perimenopause","Cyclic oestrogen + progestin (10–14 days)"],["Uterus intact, postmenopause","Continuous combined"],["Vaginal symptoms only","Local low-dose oestrogen"],["Alternative","Tibolone"]]],
["Benefits vs risks",[["Benefits","Risks"],["Relief of vasomotor symptoms","VTE, stroke"],["Prevention of osteoporosis/fractures","Breast cancer (combined, long-term)"],["Vaginal atrophy relief","Endometrial cancer (unopposed oestrogen)"],["↓ Colorectal cancer","Gall bladder disease"]]]],
{c:"HRT is the most effective treatment for menopausal symptoms; it should be individualised, include a progestin when the uterus is present, and use the lowest effective dose with regular review."});

Z("pharm4",8,"ph",["SERMs (selective oestrogen receptor modulators) act as oestrogen agonists in some tissues and antagonists in others; tamoxifen blocks oestrogen in breast (cancer), raloxifene protects bone without stimulating breast or uterus, clomiphene induces ovulation.","One key fits the oestrogen lock in every tissue, but turns it on in some rooms and blocks it in others — tissue-selective action.",["Tamoxifen: antagonist in breast, agonist in endometrium, bone, liver; ER-positive breast cancer (adjuvant 5–10 years), prevention in high-risk women; ADR — hot flushes, endometrial cancer, VTE, cataract.","Raloxifene: agonist in bone and lipids, antagonist in breast and uterus; postmenopausal osteoporosis, breast cancer risk reduction; ADR — hot flushes, VTE, leg cramps; no endometrial cancer.","Clomiphene: antagonist at hypothalamus → ↑ GnRH, FSH, LH → ovulation induction (PCOS, anovulatory infertility); ADR — ovarian hyperstimulation, multiple pregnancy, visual disturbance.","Others: toremifene, bazedoxifene, ospemifene (dyspareunia); fulvestrant is a pure antagonist (SERD)."],"SERMs: 'Tamoxifen = breast cancer, Raloxifene = bone, Clomiphene = ovulation'."],
"Selective oestrogen receptor modulators are non-steroidal compounds that bind oestrogen receptors and act as agonists or antagonists depending on the tissue, owing to differences in receptor conformation, ER-α/ER-β distribution, and availability of coactivators and corepressors. Tamoxifen: oestrogen antagonist in breast tissue; partial agonist in endometrium (endometrial hyperplasia, polyps, carcinoma), bone (preserves bone density in postmenopausal women), liver (lowers LDL, raises SHBG and clotting factors); metabolised by CYP2D6 to active endoxifen (poor metabolisers and CYP2D6 inhibitors such as paroxetine reduce efficacy); uses — oestrogen receptor-positive breast cancer (adjuvant therapy for 5–10 years in premenopausal and postmenopausal women; metastatic disease), ductal carcinoma in situ, prevention of breast cancer in high-risk women, male gynaecomastia, McCune–Albright syndrome; adverse effects — hot flushes, vaginal discharge, menstrual irregularity, endometrial carcinoma and uterine sarcoma (annual evaluation of abnormal bleeding), venous thromboembolism, stroke, cataract, tumour flare and hypercalcaemia initially, fatty liver. Raloxifene: oestrogen agonist on bone (reduces resorption, increases BMD, reduces vertebral fractures) and lipids (lowers LDL); antagonist in breast and endometrium (no endometrial stimulation); uses — treatment and prevention of postmenopausal osteoporosis, reduction of invasive breast cancer risk in postmenopausal women with osteoporosis or high risk; adverse effects — hot flushes, leg cramps, peripheral oedema, VTE, fatal stroke in women with coronary disease; does not relieve menopausal symptoms. Clomiphene citrate: antagonises oestrogen receptors in the hypothalamus, blocking negative feedback, increasing GnRH and gonadotropin secretion and inducing follicular development and ovulation; uses — anovulatory infertility including PCOS (50–150 mg/day from day 2–5 for 5 days), male hypogonadism (off-label); adverse effects — hot flushes, ovarian enlargement and cysts, ovarian hyperstimulation, multiple pregnancy (5–10%), visual blurring (stop), abdominal discomfort; letrozole now often preferred in PCOS. Others: toremifene (breast cancer), bazedoxifene (with conjugated oestrogens for menopause), ospemifene (dyspareunia from vulvovaginal atrophy), lasofoxifene. Fulvestrant is a selective oestrogen receptor degrader (pure antagonist) for advanced breast cancer.",
[["SERM tissue effects",[["Drug","Breast","Endometrium","Bone","Main use"],["Tamoxifen","Antagonist","Agonist","Agonist","ER+ breast cancer"],["Raloxifene","Antagonist","Antagonist","Agonist","Osteoporosis, breast cancer prevention"],["Clomiphene","—","—","—","Ovulation induction (hypothalamic antagonist)"],["Ospemifene","—","Weak agonist","—","Dyspareunia (vaginal agonist)"]]],
["Adverse effects",[["Drug","ADRs"],["Tamoxifen","Hot flushes, endometrial cancer, VTE, cataract"],["Raloxifene","Hot flushes, leg cramps, VTE"],["Clomiphene","OHSS, multiple pregnancy, visual disturbance"]]]],
{c:"SERMs exploit tissue-selective oestrogen receptor actions: tamoxifen for breast cancer, raloxifene for osteoporosis and clomiphene for ovulation induction, each with characteristic risks."});

Z("pharm4",9,"ph",["Letrozole is an aromatase inhibitor that blocks oestrogen production, used in breast cancer and ovulation induction; mifepristone is a progesterone (and glucocorticoid) receptor antagonist used for medical abortion.","Letrozole shuts the oestrogen factory (aromatase) in fat tissue; mifepristone blocks the progesterone 'pregnancy-maintaining' signal so the pregnancy detaches.",["Letrozole: non-steroidal aromatase inhibitor (also anastrozole; exemestane — steroidal, irreversible); postmenopausal ER+ breast cancer (adjuvant, metastatic), ovulation induction in PCOS (better than clomiphene).","Letrozole ADR: hot flushes, arthralgia, osteoporosis (bone loss), hypercholesterolaemia; not effective alone in premenopausal women (ovaries compensate).","Mifepristone: blocks progesterone receptors → decidual breakdown, cervical softening, ↑ uterine sensitivity to prostaglandins; with misoprostol for medical abortion up to 9 weeks (India MTP Act — up to 9 weeks).","Mifepristone other uses: emergency contraception, cervical ripening, fibroids, Cushing's (GR antagonism); ADR — bleeding, cramps, incomplete abortion."],"Medical abortion: 'Mifepristone 200 mg → 24–48 h → Misoprostol 800 µg'."],
"Letrozole is a potent, selective, reversible non-steroidal inhibitor of aromatase (CYP19), the enzyme converting androgens (androstenedione, testosterone) to oestrogens (oestrone, oestradiol) in peripheral tissues (adipose, muscle, breast tumour) — the main source of oestrogen in postmenopausal women; it reduces circulating oestrogen by > 95%. Other aromatase inhibitors: anastrozole (non-steroidal), exemestane (steroidal, irreversible — 'suicide inhibitor'). Uses: hormone receptor-positive breast cancer in postmenopausal women (adjuvant — superior to tamoxifen; extended adjuvant after tamoxifen; metastatic disease; neoadjuvant), in premenopausal women only with ovarian suppression (otherwise reduced feedback raises gonadotropins and ovarian oestrogen); ovulation induction in PCOS and unexplained infertility (2.5–7.5 mg on days 3–7; higher live birth rates than clomiphene, fewer multiple pregnancies, no anti-oestrogenic effect on endometrium) — off-label in many countries; gynaecomastia, endometriosis (adjunct). Adverse effects: hot flushes, arthralgia and myalgia, fatigue, osteoporosis and fractures (monitor BMD; give calcium, vitamin D, bisphosphonate), hypercholesterolaemia, vaginal dryness; less risk of endometrial cancer and VTE than tamoxifen. Mifepristone (RU-486): antiprogestin — competitive antagonist at progesterone receptors (with some partial agonist activity) and, at higher doses, glucocorticoid receptor antagonist. Effects: in early pregnancy blocks progesterone support of decidua → decidual necrosis, detachment of blastocyst, reduced hCG and corpus luteum support, softening and dilatation of cervix, increased uterine contractility and sensitivity to prostaglandins. Uses: medical termination of intrauterine pregnancy — 200 mg orally followed 24–48 hours later by misoprostol 800 µg vaginally/buccally/sublingually (or 400 µg orally), up to 9 weeks (63 days) of gestation in India (registered under MTP rules) with > 95% success; second-trimester abortion (pretreatment before misoprostol); cervical ripening and induction of labour after intrauterine fetal death; emergency contraception (10–25 mg; more effective than levonorgestrel, not approved everywhere); uterine fibroids and endometriosis; Cushing syndrome (hyperglycaemia — Korlym); meningioma (investigational). Adverse effects: vaginal bleeding (heavy, prolonged), abdominal cramps, nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, incomplete abortion requiring surgical evacuation, infection (rare), teratogenicity if pregnancy continues. Contraindications: ectopic pregnancy (must be excluded by ultrasound), chronic adrenal failure, long-term corticosteroid therapy, haemorrhagic disorders and anticoagulants, IUD in situ (remove first), porphyria, allergy.",
[["Letrozole",["Aromatase inhibitor (non-steroidal, reversible)","Postmenopausal ER+ breast cancer","Ovulation induction in PCOS","ADR: hot flushes, arthralgia, bone loss, hypercholesterolaemia"]],
["Mifepristone",["Progesterone (and glucocorticoid) receptor antagonist","Medical abortion ≤ 9 weeks with misoprostol","Emergency contraception, cervical ripening, IUFD, fibroids, Cushing's","CI: ectopic pregnancy, adrenal failure, bleeding disorders"]]],
{c:"Letrozole suppresses oestrogen synthesis for breast cancer and ovulation induction, while mifepristone antagonises progesterone and, with misoprostol, provides safe medical abortion in early pregnancy."});

Z("pharm4",10,"ph",["Oral contraceptives prevent pregnancy mainly by stopping ovulation; combined pills contain oestrogen + progestin, while progestin-only pills ('mini pills') and emergency pills are other types.","The pill fools the brain into thinking pregnancy has already happened, so it stops releasing the eggs; progestin also thickens cervical mucus as a barrier.",["Types: combined OCPs (ethinyl oestradiol + levonorgestrel/desogestrel/drospirenone — monophasic, biphasic, triphasic), progestin-only pill, emergency contraception (levonorgestrel 1.5 mg within 72 h, ulipristal), centchroman (ormeloxifene — Saheli/Chhaya, weekly non-steroidal).","MOA: ↓ GnRH, FSH, LH (no follicle development, no LH surge) → anovulation; progestin thickens cervical mucus, makes endometrium unreceptive, alters tubal motility.","Non-contraceptive benefits: regular cycles, ↓ dysmenorrhoea, menorrhagia, anaemia, ovarian and endometrial cancer, PID, benign breast disease; acne/PCOS (drospirenone, cyproterone).","ADR: nausea, breast tenderness, breakthrough bleeding, weight gain, headache, VTE, hypertension, MI/stroke (smokers > 35), cholestasis, hepatic adenoma. CI: VTE, stroke/IHD, breast cancer, liver disease, migraine with aura, smokers > 35, uncontrolled hypertension, pregnancy, < 6 weeks postpartum breastfeeding."],"Absolute CI for COC: 'Clots, Cancer (breast), Cerebral/cardiac disease, Cigarettes > 35, Cirrhosis'."],
"Oral contraceptives are steroidal (and non-steroidal) preparations taken to prevent pregnancy. Classification: (1) combined oral contraceptives — low-dose ethinyl oestradiol (20–35 µg) with a progestin (levonorgestrel, norethisterone, desogestrel, gestodene, norgestimate, drospirenone, cyproterone acetate, dienogest); monophasic (fixed dose for 21 days, then 7 days pill-free or placebo/iron), biphasic and triphasic (varying doses), extended-cycle; in India 'Mala-N' (free) and 'Mala-D' (social marketing); (2) progestin-only pills ('mini pill') — norethisterone 0.35 mg, levonorgestrel 30 µg, desogestrel 75 µg — taken daily without break; suitable for lactating women and those with contraindications to oestrogen; (3) emergency (postcoital) contraception — levonorgestrel 1.5 mg single dose within 72 hours (up to 120 h, decreasing efficacy), ulipristal acetate 30 mg within 120 h, mifepristone, Yuzpe regimen (combined pills), copper IUD; (4) centchroman (ormeloxifene) — non-steroidal SERM developed by CDRI Lucknow, 30 mg twice weekly for 3 months then weekly ('Chhaya' in national programme). Mechanism: oestrogen and progestin suppress hypothalamic GnRH and pituitary FSH (oestrogen — prevents follicle selection) and LH surge (progestin), inhibiting ovulation; progestin also thickens cervical mucus (impeding sperm penetration), makes endometrium atrophic and non-receptive to implantation, and alters tubal motility. Efficacy: > 99% with perfect use (Pearl index 0.1–1), ~ 91–93% typical use. Uses: contraception; non-contraceptive — menstrual regulation, dysmenorrhoea, menorrhagia, premenstrual syndrome, endometriosis, PCOS (cyproterone/drospirenone — anti-androgenic for acne and hirsutism), functional ovarian cysts, reduction of ovarian and endometrial cancer risk (by 50%), benign breast disease, PID, iron deficiency anaemia, ectopic pregnancy. Adverse effects: minor — nausea, vomiting, headache, breast tenderness, breakthrough bleeding/spotting, amenorrhoea, weight gain, mood changes, chloasma, acne (androgenic progestins); serious — venous thromboembolism (oestrogen increases clotting factors; higher with drospirenone/desogestrel), arterial thrombosis (MI, stroke) especially in smokers over 35, hypertension, glucose intolerance, cholestatic jaundice, gall stones, benign hepatic adenoma, slight increase in breast and cervical cancer risk; progestin-only pills — irregular bleeding, functional ovarian cysts, ectopic pregnancy risk if failure. Drug interactions: enzyme inducers (rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, griseofulvin, St John's wort, some antiretrovirals) cause contraceptive failure; broad-spectrum antibiotics (theoretical). Contraindications (WHO MEC category 4 for COCs): history of DVT/PE, known thrombophilia, ischaemic heart disease, stroke, complicated valvular disease, migraine with aura, current breast cancer, severe liver disease or hepatic tumour, smoking ≥ 15 cigarettes/day aged ≥ 35, uncontrolled hypertension (≥ 160/100), diabetes with vascular complications, major surgery with prolonged immobilisation, breastfeeding < 6 weeks postpartum, pregnancy.",
[["Types",[["Type","Composition","Notes"],["Combined OCP","EE 20–35 µg + progestin","21/7 regimen; most effective"],["Progestin-only pill","Norethisterone/levonorgestrel/desogestrel","Lactation, oestrogen CI; strict timing"],["Emergency","Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg; ulipristal 30 mg","Within 72/120 h"],["Centchroman","Ormeloxifene 30 mg","Weekly; non-steroidal (India)"]]],
["Mechanism",["↓ GnRH, FSH, LH → no follicle maturation, no LH surge → anovulation","Thick cervical mucus","Atrophic endometrium","Altered tubal motility"]],
["Adverse effects",[["Minor","Serious"],["Nausea, headache","Venous thromboembolism"],["Breast tenderness","MI, stroke (smokers > 35)"],["Breakthrough bleeding","Hypertension"],["Weight gain, mood change","Cholestatic jaundice, hepatic adenoma"]]],
["Contraindications",["History of VTE, stroke, IHD","Breast cancer","Active liver disease","Migraine with aura","Smoker ≥ 35 years","Uncontrolled hypertension","Pregnancy","< 6 weeks postpartum breastfeeding"]]],
{c:"Oral contraceptives are highly effective and offer many non-contraceptive benefits; careful screening for contraindications and attention to drug interactions ensure safe use."});

Z("pharm4",11,"ph",["Tocolytics are drugs that relax the uterus and suppress preterm labour, buying time (48 h) for corticosteroids to mature the fetal lungs and for transfer to a NICU-equipped centre.","Tocolytics press pause on premature contractions — not to prevent birth indefinitely, but to gain 2 days for the baby's lungs to be prepared.",["Nifedipine (calcium channel blocker): first-line in many guidelines; oral; ADR — hypotension, tachycardia, headache.","Atosiban (oxytocin receptor antagonist): fewer maternal side effects; costly.","β2 agonists (ritodrine, terbutaline, isoxsuprine): ADR — tachycardia, pulmonary oedema, hyperglycaemia, hypokalaemia.","Indomethacin (COX inhibitor) < 32 weeks — risk of ductus closure, oligohydramnios; magnesium sulphate — neuroprotection < 32 weeks (weak tocolytic); progesterone for prevention. CI: chorioamnionitis, abruption, severe pre-eclampsia, fetal distress/death."],"Tocolysis goal: '48 hours for steroids (betamethasone) + transfer'."],
"Tocolytics (uterine relaxants) are drugs that inhibit myometrial contractions, used to delay preterm labour (onset between 20 and 37 weeks) for 48 hours to allow administration of antenatal corticosteroids (betamethasone 12 mg IM two doses 24 h apart or dexamethasone 6 mg four doses) for fetal lung maturation, magnesium sulphate for neuroprotection (< 32 weeks) and in-utero transfer to a centre with neonatal intensive care; they do not improve neonatal outcome by themselves when used longer. Classes: (1) calcium channel blockers — nifedipine (oral 20 mg loading, then 10–20 mg 4–6 hourly): inhibit Ca²⁺ influx into myometrial cells; first-line in many guidelines (effective, oral, cheap); adverse effects — maternal hypotension, tachycardia, flushing, headache, dizziness; avoid with magnesium (neuromuscular blockade) and in cardiac disease; (2) oxytocin receptor antagonist — atosiban (IV): competitive antagonist at oxytocin and vasopressin V1a receptors; as effective as β-agonists with fewer side effects; expensive; (3) β2-adrenergic agonists — ritodrine, terbutaline, salbutamol, isoxsuprine (commonly used in India): increase cAMP, relax myometrium; adverse effects — maternal tachycardia, palpitations, tremor, hypotension, chest pain, arrhythmias, pulmonary oedema, hyperglycaemia, hypokalaemia; fetal tachycardia, neonatal hypoglycaemia; contraindicated in cardiac disease, diabetes, hyperthyroidism; (4) prostaglandin synthesis inhibitors — indomethacin (< 32 weeks, for ≤ 48–72 h): reduce PG-mediated contractions; fetal risks — premature closure of ductus arteriosus, oligohydramnios (reduced fetal urine), necrotising enterocolitis, intraventricular haemorrhage; (5) magnesium sulphate — competes with calcium; weak tocolytic, used mainly for fetal neuroprotection (reduces cerebral palsy) and eclampsia; toxicity — loss of patellar reflex, respiratory depression (antidote calcium gluconate); (6) others — nitroglycerin (patch), progesterone (vaginal/17-OHPC for prevention of preterm birth in women with short cervix or previous preterm birth), ethanol (obsolete). Contraindications to tocolysis: gestational age > 34 weeks, intrauterine fetal death, lethal fetal anomaly, non-reassuring fetal status, chorioamnionitis, severe pre-eclampsia/eclampsia, significant antepartum haemorrhage (placental abruption, placenta praevia with bleeding), maternal haemodynamic instability, advanced cervical dilatation (> 4–5 cm), contraindication to the specific drug.",
[["Tocolytics",[["Class","Drug","Key ADRs"],["Calcium channel blocker","Nifedipine","Hypotension, tachycardia, headache"],["Oxytocin antagonist","Atosiban","Minimal; costly"],["β2 agonists","Ritodrine, terbutaline, isoxsuprine","Tachycardia, pulmonary oedema, hyperglycaemia, hypokalaemia"],["COX inhibitor","Indomethacin","Ductus closure, oligohydramnios"],["Magnesium sulphate","MgSO4","Respiratory depression; neuroprotection"],["Progestogens","Progesterone, 17-OHPC","Prevention, not acute tocolysis"]]],
["Contraindications",["> 34 weeks","Chorioamnionitis","Fetal death or distress, lethal anomaly","Severe pre-eclampsia","Abruption/significant bleeding","Advanced labour"]]],
{c:"Tocolytics delay preterm labour briefly to allow antenatal steroids and transfer; nifedipine or atosiban are preferred, and contraindications such as infection and abruption must be excluded."});

Z("pharm4",12,"ph",["Bisphosphonates are drugs that stick to bone and switch off osteoclasts (bone-resorbing cells), increasing bone density and reducing fractures; they treat osteoporosis, Paget's disease, hypercalcaemia of malignancy and bone metastases.","They act like a coating on the bone surface that poisons the bone-eating cells (osteoclasts) when they try to chew it.",["Classification: non-nitrogenous (etidronate, clodronate, tiludronate) and nitrogen-containing (alendronate, risedronate, ibandronate, pamidronate, zoledronate — more potent).","MOA: bind hydroxyapatite; taken up by osteoclasts; N-BPs inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase (mevalonate pathway) → impaired osteoclast function and apoptosis; non-N-BPs form toxic ATP analogues.","Uses: postmenopausal/steroid-induced/male osteoporosis (alendronate 70 mg weekly, zoledronate 5 mg IV yearly), Paget's disease, hypercalcaemia of malignancy (zoledronate, pamidronate IV), bone metastases and myeloma, osteogenesis imperfecta.","ADR: oesophagitis/ulcers (take with full glass of water, empty stomach, stay upright 30 min), hypocalcaemia, osteonecrosis of jaw, atypical femoral fractures, acute-phase reaction (IV), renal toxicity; avoid in eGFR < 30–35. Poor oral bioavailability (< 1%)."],"Oral bisphosphonate rule: 'Empty stomach, plain water, upright 30 min'."],
"Bisphosphonates are synthetic analogues of pyrophosphate in which a P–C–P bond replaces P–O–P, making them resistant to hydrolysis; they bind avidly to hydroxyapatite at sites of active bone remodelling. Classification: first-generation non-nitrogen-containing — etidronate, clodronate, tiludronate; second-generation nitrogen-containing (aminobisphosphonates) — pamidronate, alendronate, ibandronate; third-generation (heterocyclic nitrogen) — risedronate, zoledronic acid (most potent). Mechanism: during resorption bisphosphonates are released and internalised by osteoclasts; nitrogen-containing agents inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway, preventing prenylation of small GTPases (Ras, Rho, Rab) needed for osteoclast ruffled border formation, cytoskeletal organisation and survival, leading to loss of resorptive function and apoptosis; non-nitrogenous agents are metabolised into cytotoxic non-hydrolysable ATP analogues inducing osteoclast apoptosis; they also inhibit osteoclast recruitment. Net effect: reduced bone turnover, increased bone mineral density, reduced vertebral and non-vertebral fractures. Pharmacokinetics: very poor oral absorption (< 1%), further reduced by food, calcium, iron, antacids, coffee, juice; about half the absorbed dose deposited in bone where it persists for years (long skeletal half-life), rest excreted unchanged by kidney. Uses: postmenopausal osteoporosis (alendronate 70 mg once weekly, risedronate 35 mg weekly, ibandronate 150 mg monthly or 3 mg IV quarterly, zoledronic acid 5 mg IV once yearly), glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis, osteoporosis in men; Paget disease of bone (zoledronate single infusion, risedronate, alendronate); hypercalcaemia of malignancy (zoledronic acid 4 mg IV or pamidronate 60–90 mg IV with hydration); osteolytic bone metastases (breast, prostate, lung) and multiple myeloma (reduce skeletal-related events, bone pain); osteogenesis imperfecta in children; fibrous dysplasia; heterotopic ossification (etidronate). Administration of oral agents: take first thing in the morning on an empty stomach with a full glass (200 mL) of plain water, remain upright for at least 30 minutes (60 for ibandronate) and avoid food, drinks and other medicines for this period; ensure adequate calcium and vitamin D. Adverse effects: oral — oesophageal irritation, oesophagitis, ulcers, dysphagia, dyspepsia, nausea; IV — acute-phase reaction (fever, myalgia, flu-like symptoms after first infusion), hypocalcaemia (correct vitamin D first), nephrotoxicity (dose adjust, infuse over ≥ 15 minutes), ocular inflammation (uveitis, scleritis); with long-term or high-dose use — osteonecrosis of the jaw (mainly with high IV doses in cancer patients after dental extraction — dental check before therapy), atypical subtrochanteric femoral fractures (consider drug holiday after 3–5 years), atrial fibrillation (zoledronate), musculoskeletal pain. Contraindications: hypocalcaemia, severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance < 30–35 mL/min), oesophageal abnormalities delaying emptying (stricture, achalasia), inability to stand or sit upright for 30 minutes (oral), pregnancy.",
[["Classification",[["Generation","Drugs","Relative potency"],["First (non-N)","Etidronate, clodronate, tiludronate","1–10"],["Second (N)","Pamidronate, alendronate, ibandronate","100–1000"],["Third (heterocyclic N)","Risedronate, zoledronate","5000–10,000"]]],
["Uses and doses",[["Use","Drug"],["Postmenopausal osteoporosis","Alendronate 70 mg weekly; zoledronate 5 mg IV yearly"],["Paget disease","Zoledronate 5 mg IV, risedronate"],["Hypercalcaemia of malignancy","Zoledronate 4 mg IV, pamidronate"],["Bone metastases, myeloma","Zoledronate monthly"]]],
["Adverse effects",["Oesophagitis, ulcers (oral)","Acute-phase reaction (IV)","Hypocalcaemia","Renal toxicity","Osteonecrosis of jaw","Atypical femoral fractures","Uveitis, scleritis"]]],
{i:"Bisphosphonates are the most widely used anti-resorptive drugs; their potency increases from non-nitrogenous to heterocyclic nitrogen-containing compounds.",f:"Bisphosphonate → binds hydroxyapatite → ingested by osteoclast\n → N-BP: inhibits FPP synthase → no prenylation of GTPases\n → osteoclast dysfunction & apoptosis → ↓ resorption → ↑ BMD, ↓ fractures",c:"Bisphosphonates inhibit osteoclast-mediated resorption and are first-line for osteoporosis and key in malignant bone disease; correct oral administration and monitoring for rare jaw and femoral complications ensure safety."});

Z("pharm5",0,"ph",["Skeletal muscle relaxants reduce muscle tone; they are peripherally acting (neuromuscular blockers used in anaesthesia, and dantrolene acting on muscle) or centrally acting (for spasticity and muscle spasm).","Peripheral relaxants cut the signal at the nerve–muscle junction or inside the muscle; central relaxants calm the spinal reflexes that keep muscles tight.",["Peripherally acting — neuromuscular blockers: non-depolarising (competitive) — d-tubocurarine, pancuronium, vecuronium, atracurium, cisatracurium, rocuronium, mivacurium; depolarising — succinylcholine.","Directly acting: dantrolene (blocks Ca²⁺ release via RyR1), quinine, botulinum toxin (presynaptic ACh release block).","Centrally acting: mephenesin congeners (chlorzoxazone, methocarbamol), benzodiazepines (diazepam), GABA-B agonist baclofen, α2 agonist tizanidine, thiocolchicoside, tolperisone.","Uses: NM blockers — surgery, intubation, ECT, ventilation; central — spasticity (cerebral palsy, MS, spinal injury), muscle spasms, tetanus."],"NM blockers: 'Sux = depolarising; -curonium/-curium = competitive'."],
"Skeletal muscle relaxants reduce skeletal muscle tone. Classification: (I) peripherally acting — (A) neuromuscular blocking agents: (a) non-depolarising (competitive) blockers — long-acting: d-tubocurarine, pancuronium, doxacurium, pipecuronium; intermediate-acting: vecuronium, atracurium, cisatracurium, rocuronium; short-acting: mivacurium; (b) depolarising blockers — succinylcholine (suxamethonium), decamethonium; (B) directly acting agents — dantrolene sodium, quinine; (C) presynaptic — botulinum toxin A (inhibits ACh release; for dystonias, spasticity, strabismus), aminoglycosides, magnesium; (II) centrally acting — (a) mephenesin congeners: mephenesin, carisoprodol, chlorzoxazone, chlormezanone, methocarbamol; (b) benzodiazepines: diazepam and others (GABA-A facilitation in spinal cord); (c) GABA mimetic: baclofen (GABA-B agonist), thiocolchicoside (GABA-A); (d) α2-adrenergic agonist: tizanidine; (e) others: tolperisone, gabapentin, cyclobenzaprine. Neuromuscular blockers act at the nicotinic NM receptors of the motor end plate; competitive blockers prevent ACh binding (reversed by neostigmine, sugammadex for rocuronium/vecuronium), while succinylcholine causes persistent depolarisation (phase I block with fasciculations), later desensitisation (phase II). They have no CNS effects and are used only under anaesthesia with ventilatory support. Uses: adjuvant to general anaesthesia for muscle relaxation during surgery, endotracheal intubation (succinylcholine, rocuronium — rapid sequence), mechanical ventilation in ICU, electroconvulsive therapy (to prevent fractures), tetanus and status epilepticus with ventilatory support, orthopaedic manipulations, laryngoscopy and bronchoscopy. Centrally acting relaxants reduce tone by depressing polysynaptic spinal reflexes without loss of consciousness; uses — spasticity due to cerebral palsy, multiple sclerosis, spinal cord injury, stroke (baclofen, tizanidine, diazepam, dantrolene); acute muscle spasms due to sprains, back pain, torticollis (chlorzoxazone, methocarbamol, thiocolchicoside with analgesics); tetanus (diazepam); orthopaedic manipulations; anxiety with muscle tension. Adverse effects of central agents: sedation, dizziness, weakness, dependence (carisoprodol), hepatotoxicity (tizanidine, dantrolene), withdrawal seizures and hallucinations on abrupt stopping of baclofen.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs"],["Non-depolarising NM blockers","d-Tubocurarine, pancuronium, vecuronium, atracurium, cisatracurium, rocuronium, mivacurium"],["Depolarising NM blocker","Succinylcholine"],["Direct-acting","Dantrolene, quinine"],["Presynaptic","Botulinum toxin"],["Centrally acting","Baclofen, tizanidine, diazepam, chlorzoxazone, methocarbamol, thiocolchicoside, tolperisone"]]],
["Peripheral vs central",[["Feature","Peripheral (NM blockers)","Central"],["Site","NM junction","Spinal cord, brainstem"],["Consciousness","Unaffected but given under GA","Mild sedation"],["Respiration","Paralysed (need ventilation)","Not affected at usual doses"],["Route","IV","Oral"],["Uses","Surgery, intubation","Spasticity, spasms"]]]],
{c:"Muscle relaxants are chosen by site of action: neuromuscular blockers for anaesthesia with ventilatory support, dantrolene and central agents for spasticity and spasms."});

Z("pharm5",1,"ph",["d-Tubocurarine is the prototype non-depolarising (competitive) neuromuscular blocker: it occupies nicotinic receptors at the motor end-plate so acetylcholine cannot act, causing flaccid paralysis.","It sits in the acetylcholine 'lock' without turning it, so muscles cannot be activated; flooding with acetylcholine (neostigmine) pushes it out.",["Source: curare (arrow poison); quaternary — not absorbed orally, does not cross BBB or placenta much.","Sequence of paralysis: small rapid muscles (eyes, fingers) → limbs → trunk → intercostals → diaphragm (last); recovery in reverse.","Causes histamine release → hypotension, bronchospasm, flushing; ganglion blockade → hypotension.","Reversed by neostigmine (with atropine/glycopyrrolate); potentiated by inhalational anaesthetics, aminoglycosides, Mg²⁺, hypothermia; largely replaced by safer agents (vecuronium, atracurium, rocuronium)."],"d-TC = 'Diaphragm last, histamine release, neostigmine reverses'."],
"d-Tubocurarine, an alkaloid from the South American arrow poison curare, is the prototype non-depolarising (competitive) neuromuscular blocker. Mechanism: competes with acetylcholine for nicotinic (Nm) receptors at the motor end-plate without activating them, reducing end-plate potential below threshold, producing flaccid paralysis; also blocks prejunctional receptors (fade on tetanic and train-of-four stimulation). Sequence: small, rapidly contracting muscles of eyes, fingers, face and toes are paralysed first, followed by limbs, neck, trunk, intercostals and finally the diaphragm; recovery occurs in reverse order. No fasciculations. Pharmacokinetics: quaternary ammonium compound — not absorbed orally, given IV, onset 4–6 minutes, duration 30–60 minutes (long-acting), does not cross BBB and crosses placenta poorly; excreted mainly unchanged in urine (prolonged in renal failure). Other actions: histamine release (hypotension, bronchospasm, flushing, increased secretions), ganglion blockade (hypotension), no direct CNS effect. Interactions: potentiated by inhalational anaesthetics (isoflurane, sevoflurane), aminoglycosides, polymyxins, clindamycin, magnesium sulphate, calcium channel blockers, quinidine, hypokalaemia, hypothermia, acidosis, myasthenia gravis (very sensitive); antagonised by anticholinesterases (neostigmine, edrophonium — reversal, given with atropine or glycopyrrolate), hyperkalaemia. Uses: muscle relaxation during surgery and controlled ventilation — now replaced by agents with fewer cardiovascular effects and more predictable kinetics (vecuronium, rocuronium, atracurium/cisatracurium — Hofmann elimination, safe in renal and hepatic failure); historically in tetanus and ECT. Adverse effects: hypotension, bronchospasm (avoid in asthmatics), tachycardia, respiratory paralysis (prolonged apnoea), malignant hyperthermia is not associated.",
[["Features",["Competitive antagonist at Nm receptors","Paralysis: small muscles first, diaphragm last","No fasciculations","Histamine release, ganglion block → hypotension, bronchospasm","Reversed by neostigmine + atropine","Potentiated by inhalational agents, aminoglycosides, Mg²⁺"]],
["Comparison of non-depolarising blockers",[["Drug","Duration","Notes"],["d-Tubocurarine","Long","Histamine release, hypotension"],["Pancuronium","Long","Vagolytic → tachycardia"],["Vecuronium","Intermediate","Cardiostable"],["Atracurium","Intermediate","Hofmann elimination; histamine"],["Cisatracurium","Intermediate","Hofmann; no histamine"],["Rocuronium","Intermediate","Rapid onset; sugammadex reversal"]]]],
{c:"d-Tubocurarine illustrates competitive neuromuscular blockade; its histamine-releasing and ganglion-blocking effects led to its replacement by safer modern agents."});

Z("pharm5",2,"ph",["Succinylcholine is a depolarising neuromuscular blocker with very rapid onset and short action, used for rapid intubation; it first causes muscle twitching (fasciculations), then paralysis.","It acts like acetylcholine that won't leave: the muscle fires once (fasciculation) and then stays switched off because the end-plate cannot reset.",["Two ACh molecules joined; acts on Nm receptors → persistent depolarisation (phase I block), later desensitisation (phase II).","Onset < 1 min, duration 5–10 min; hydrolysed by plasma pseudocholinesterase (butyrylcholinesterase).","ADR: fasciculations and postoperative myalgia, hyperkalaemia (burns, crush, denervation, spinal injury), malignant hyperthermia (with halothane — treat with dantrolene), bradycardia, ↑ IOP, ↑ intragastric and intracranial pressure, prolonged apnoea in pseudocholinesterase deficiency.","Neostigmine does not reverse (worsens phase I); uses — rapid sequence intubation, ECT, laryngoscopy, short procedures."],"Sux: 'Short, Sudden, Sux causes K⁺ rise & MH'."],
"Succinylcholine (suxamethonium) is a depolarising neuromuscular blocker consisting of two acetylcholine molecules linked back to back. Mechanism: acts as an agonist at nicotinic Nm receptors, producing depolarisation of the motor end-plate; unlike ACh it is not hydrolysed by acetylcholinesterase, so depolarisation persists. Phase I (depolarising) block: initial transient muscle fasciculations followed by flaccid paralysis as the depolarised end-plate makes adjacent membrane inexcitable (sodium channels inactivated); no fade with train-of-four; potentiated by anticholinesterases. Phase II (desensitising) block: with prolonged exposure or high doses, membrane repolarises but receptors become desensitised, resembling a non-depolarising block (fade present, partially reversed by neostigmine). Pharmacokinetics: IV, onset 30–60 seconds (fastest), duration 5–10 minutes; rapidly hydrolysed by plasma pseudocholinesterase (butyrylcholinesterase) to succinylmonocholine and choline; prolonged action in genetically atypical or deficient pseudocholinesterase (dibucaine number low), liver disease, pregnancy, malnutrition, organophosphate exposure, neostigmine use. Uses: rapid sequence induction and endotracheal intubation, laryngoscopy, bronchoscopy, oesophagoscopy, electroconvulsive therapy (prevent fractures), short orthopaedic manipulations, laryngospasm. Adverse effects: muscle fasciculations and postoperative myalgia (pretreatment with small dose of non-depolariser), hyperkalaemia (K⁺ efflux; dangerous in burns, massive trauma, crush injury, denervation, spinal cord injury, stroke, Guillain–Barré, prolonged immobilisation, muscular dystrophy — cardiac arrest), cardiac arrhythmias — bradycardia (muscarinic stimulation, especially with repeat doses and in children; prevented by atropine), tachycardia; malignant hyperthermia (with halothane in genetically susceptible — RYR1 mutation: rigidity, hyperthermia, hypercarbia, acidosis, hyperkalaemia; treat with IV dantrolene, cooling); masseter spasm; prolonged apnoea (scoline apnoea) in pseudocholinesterase deficiency (manage with ventilation, fresh frozen plasma); raised intraocular pressure (avoid in open eye injury), intragastric pressure (aspiration risk), intracranial pressure; histamine release; myoglobinuria. Contraindications: history or family history of malignant hyperthermia, hyperkalaemia, burns/crush/denervation injuries after 24–48 h, neuromuscular diseases (muscular dystrophy), open globe injury, known pseudocholinesterase deficiency. Neostigmine cannot reverse phase I block (it inhibits pseudocholinesterase and prolongs action).",
[["Depolarising vs non-depolarising",[["Feature","Succinylcholine","d-Tubocurarine"],["Mechanism","Persistent depolarisation","Competitive antagonism"],["Fasciculations","Present","Absent"],["Onset/duration","< 1 min / 5–10 min","4–6 min / 30–60 min"],["Metabolism","Pseudocholinesterase","Renal excretion"],["Neostigmine","Potentiates phase I","Reverses"],["Train-of-four fade","No (phase I)","Yes"],["Special ADR","Hyperkalaemia, MH, myalgia","Histamine release, hypotension"]]],
["Adverse effects",["Fasciculations, myalgia","Hyperkalaemia","Malignant hyperthermia","Bradycardia","↑ IOP, ↑ ICP, ↑ intragastric pressure","Prolonged apnoea (pseudocholinesterase deficiency)"]]],
{c:"Succinylcholine provides the fastest, shortest neuromuscular block for intubation, but its risks of hyperkalaemia, malignant hyperthermia and prolonged apnoea require careful patient selection."});

Z("pharm5",3,"ph",["Pancuronium is a long-acting, non-depolarising steroidal neuromuscular blocker that does not release histamine but blocks cardiac vagal receptors, causing tachycardia.","A steroid-structured competitive blocker: steady, long paralysis without histamine-induced hypotension, but it speeds the heart.",["Competitive Nm antagonist; ~5 times more potent than d-TC; duration 60–90 min.","No histamine release, no ganglion block; vagolytic (M2) → tachycardia, ↑ BP.","Excreted mainly by kidneys — prolonged in renal failure; partly hepatic.","Uses: long surgeries, ICU ventilation (now largely replaced by vecuronium/rocuronium); reversal — neostigmine + glycopyrrolate."],"Pancuronium = 'Pumps up heart rate' (vagolytic)."],
"Pancuronium is a synthetic bisquaternary aminosteroid non-depolarising neuromuscular blocker. Mechanism: competitive antagonism of acetylcholine at nicotinic receptors of the motor end-plate. It is about 5 times more potent than d-tubocurarine, with onset 3–5 minutes and long duration (60–90 minutes). Unlike d-tubocurarine it does not release histamine or block autonomic ganglia, so it does not cause hypotension or bronchospasm; however, it has vagolytic (cardiac M2 muscarinic blocking) action and inhibits noradrenaline reuptake, producing tachycardia and mild rise in blood pressure and cardiac output — useful in patients where hypotension is undesirable but problematic in coronary artery disease. Pharmacokinetics: given IV; partly metabolised in liver to 3-OH-pancuronium (active); 80% excreted unchanged in urine — prolonged block in renal failure and elderly; cumulative with repeated doses. Uses: maintenance of muscle relaxation during long surgical procedures, controlled ventilation in ICU (now largely replaced by vecuronium, rocuronium and cisatracurium because of residual postoperative paralysis); cardiac surgery where tachycardia may offset bradycardia from opioids. Interactions and reversal: as for other competitive blockers — potentiated by volatile anaesthetics, aminoglycosides, magnesium; reversed by neostigmine with glycopyrrolate; sugammadex is less effective than for rocuronium/vecuronium. Adverse effects: tachycardia, hypertension, arrhythmias, residual neuromuscular blockade with postoperative respiratory complications, prolonged paralysis in renal failure.",
[["Comparison with other aminosteroids",[["Drug","Duration","Vagolytic","Elimination"],["Pancuronium","Long","Yes (tachycardia)","Renal"],["Vecuronium","Intermediate","No","Hepatic/biliary"],["Rocuronium","Intermediate (rapid onset)","Mild","Hepatic"]]]],
{c:"Pancuronium is a potent, long-acting competitive blocker without histamine release but with vagolytic tachycardia; renal elimination limits its use in kidney failure."});

Z("pharm5",4,"ph",["Dantrolene is a skeletal muscle relaxant that acts directly inside the muscle, blocking calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum; it is the life-saving treatment for malignant hyperthermia.","Dantrolene shuts the muscle's internal calcium tap (ryanodine receptor), so the muscle cannot contract excessively.",["MOA: blocks RyR1 channels → ↓ Ca²⁺ release → ↓ excitation–contraction coupling; no effect on NM junction or cardiac muscle significantly.","Uses: malignant hyperthermia (2.5 mg/kg IV, repeated up to 10 mg/kg), neuroleptic malignant syndrome, spasticity (spinal injury, MS, cerebral palsy, stroke), heat stroke (unproven), serotonin syndrome (not first).","Oral for spasticity; IV for emergencies.","ADR: muscle weakness, drowsiness, dizziness, diarrhoea, hepatotoxicity with long-term oral use (monitor LFT)."],"Malignant hyperthermia = 'Dantrolene Dash' (give immediately)."],
"Dantrolene is a directly acting skeletal muscle relaxant (hydantoin derivative). Mechanism: binds the ryanodine receptor type 1 (RyR1) calcium release channel of the sarcoplasmic reticulum in skeletal muscle, inhibiting release of Ca²⁺ during excitation–contraction coupling, thereby reducing the force of contraction; it does not affect neuromuscular transmission or electrical activity of the muscle membrane, has minimal effect on cardiac and smooth muscle (different RyR isoforms), and no central action at therapeutic doses (some sedation). Pharmacokinetics: oral absorption about 70% (for spasticity), IV for acute use; metabolised in liver; t½ 8–12 h. Uses: (1) malignant hyperthermia — a pharmacogenetic disorder (RYR1/CACNA1S mutation) triggered by volatile anaesthetics (halothane, sevoflurane) and succinylcholine, causing uncontrolled Ca²⁺ release, muscle rigidity, hypermetabolism, hyperthermia, hypercarbia, tachycardia, metabolic and respiratory acidosis, hyperkalaemia, rhabdomyolysis; treatment — stop triggering agents, hyperventilate with 100% O2, IV dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg rapidly, repeated every 5–10 minutes up to 10 mg/kg (or more) until symptoms subside, then 1 mg/kg 6-hourly for 24–48 h; cooling, correction of hyperkalaemia and acidosis, maintain urine output; mortality reduced from 80% to < 5%; (2) neuroleptic malignant syndrome (with bromocriptine) and severe serotonin syndrome (adjunct); (3) chronic spasticity due to upper motor neuron lesions — spinal cord injury, multiple sclerosis, cerebral palsy, stroke (oral 25–100 mg QID); does not reduce spasticity of muscle origin preferentially and may worsen function due to weakness; (4) heat stroke and ecstasy (MDMA) hyperthermia (limited evidence). Adverse effects: generalised muscle weakness, drowsiness, dizziness, malaise, diarrhoea, nausea; hepatotoxicity (dose and duration related, potentially fatal; more in women > 35 years and with oestrogens) — monitor liver function, avoid long-term > 400 mg/day; phlebitis with IV (alkaline); pleural effusion. A newer formulation (Ryanodex) allows faster reconstitution.",
[["Malignant hyperthermia management",["Stop volatile agents and succinylcholine","100% oxygen, hyperventilate","IV dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg, repeat to 10 mg/kg","Active cooling","Treat hyperkalaemia, acidosis, arrhythmias","Maintain urine output (myoglobinuria)"]],
["Uses",["Malignant hyperthermia","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Spasticity (spinal injury, MS, CP, stroke)","Serotonin syndrome (adjunct)"]]],
{c:"Dantrolene, by blocking sarcoplasmic calcium release, is the specific antidote for malignant hyperthermia and a useful oral agent for spasticity, with hepatotoxicity requiring monitoring."});

Z("pharm5",5,"ph",["Local anaesthetics reversibly block nerve conduction in a limited area by blocking sodium channels, producing loss of sensation without loss of consciousness.","They plug the sodium gates on nerve fibres so the pain message cannot travel; thin pain fibres are blocked first.",["Classification: esters (cocaine, procaine, chloroprocaine, tetracaine, benzocaine) — metabolised by plasma esterase, PABA allergy; amides (lignocaine, bupivacaine, ropivacaine, prilocaine, mepivacaine, articaine) — hepatic metabolism, rarely allergic.","MOA: un-ionised base crosses membrane; ionised form blocks voltage-gated Na⁺ channels from inside (use-dependent); order of block — small myelinated (B, Aδ) and C fibres → pain, temperature → touch → motor.","Techniques/uses: surface (topical), infiltration, nerve block, field block, spinal, epidural, IV regional (Bier block); lignocaine also antiarrhythmic.","ADR/toxicity: CNS — circumoral numbness, tinnitus, convulsions, coma; CVS — bradycardia, hypotension, arrhythmia, cardiac arrest (bupivacaine — cardiotoxic; treat with lipid emulsion); methaemoglobinaemia (prilocaine, benzocaine); allergy (esters). Adrenaline added to prolong action (not in end-arteries)."],"Amides have two 'i's in name (lIdocaIne, bupIvacaIne); esters have one."],
"Local anaesthetics are drugs that, applied locally, reversibly block nerve conduction, causing loss of sensation (and motor function) in a circumscribed area without loss of consciousness. Chemistry: aromatic lipophilic ring + intermediate chain (ester or amide link) + hydrophilic amine group; weak bases (pKa 7.6–9) formulated as hydrochloride salts. Classification: (A) esters — cocaine, procaine, chloroprocaine, tetracaine (amethocaine), benzocaine (surface only); (B) amides — lignocaine (lidocaine), bupivacaine, levobupivacaine, ropivacaine, prilocaine, mepivacaine, articaine, dibucaine; by use — surface (benzocaine, lignocaine, tetracaine, oxethazaine), injectable (lignocaine, bupivacaine, procaine), spinal (bupivacaine heavy, lignocaine); by duration — short (procaine, chloroprocaine), intermediate (lignocaine, prilocaine, mepivacaine), long (bupivacaine, ropivacaine, tetracaine, dibucaine). Mechanism: the un-ionised base diffuses across the nerve sheath and membrane; inside the axoplasm the ionised cationic form binds to a site in the inner vestibule of voltage-gated Na⁺ channels (α subunit), stabilising the inactivated state, preventing Na⁺ influx, generation and propagation of action potentials; block is use- (frequency-) dependent. Order of blockade: small-diameter myelinated autonomic (B) fibres and small unmyelinated C and Aδ fibres are blocked first → loss of pain, temperature, then touch, deep pressure, and finally motor (Aα) function; recovery in reverse order. Factors: pH (acidic infected tissue reduces efficacy — more ionised), lipid solubility (potency), protein binding (duration), pKa (onset), vasodilatation (most LAs except cocaine, ropivacaine). Adrenaline (1:200,000) is added to cause vasoconstriction, reducing systemic absorption and toxicity, prolonging duration, providing a bloodless field; contraindicated in end-arteries (fingers, toes, penis, ear, nose), hypertension, ischaemic heart disease, thyrotoxicosis. Pharmacokinetics: esters hydrolysed by plasma pseudocholinesterase (short t½; PABA metabolite → allergy); amides metabolised in liver (prolonged in liver disease, heart failure); prilocaine metabolite o-toluidine causes methaemoglobinaemia. Techniques: surface/topical anaesthesia (mucous membranes, eye, skin — EMLA cream), infiltration, field block, nerve block (brachial plexus, dental), spinal (subarachnoid) anaesthesia, epidural (including caudal; labour analgesia), intravenous regional anaesthesia (Bier block — lignocaine/prilocaine, never bupivacaine), tumescent anaesthesia. Systemic uses: lignocaine as class IB antiarrhythmic (ventricular arrhythmias), for neuropathic pain, to suppress intubation responses. Adverse effects/systemic toxicity (from overdose or inadvertent IV injection): CNS — light-headedness, circumoral and tongue numbness, metallic taste, tinnitus, visual disturbances, muscle twitching, restlessness, convulsions, followed by CNS depression, coma, respiratory arrest; CVS — myocardial depression, bradycardia, hypotension, AV block, ventricular arrhythmias and cardiovascular collapse (bupivacaine most cardiotoxic — binds cardiac Na⁺ channels avidly, resistant to resuscitation); management of LAST — stop injection, airway, oxygen, benzodiazepines for seizures, CPR, 20% intravenous lipid emulsion (1.5 mL/kg bolus then infusion); local — tissue irritation, neurotoxicity (cauda equina with high concentration lignocaine spinal), delayed wound healing; hypersensitivity (mainly esters); methaemoglobinaemia (prilocaine, benzocaine — treat with methylene blue); spinal anaesthesia complications — hypotension, bradycardia, post-dural puncture headache, urinary retention, high/total spinal block. Maximum doses: lignocaine 4.5 mg/kg (7 mg/kg with adrenaline), bupivacaine 2 mg/kg.",
[["Classification",[["Type","Drugs","Metabolism","Allergy"],["Esters","Cocaine, procaine, chloroprocaine, tetracaine, benzocaine","Plasma esterases","More (PABA)"],["Amides","Lignocaine, bupivacaine, ropivacaine, prilocaine, mepivacaine, articaine","Liver","Rare"]]],
["Duration",[["Duration","Drugs"],["Short","Procaine, chloroprocaine"],["Intermediate","Lignocaine, prilocaine, mepivacaine"],["Long","Bupivacaine, ropivacaine, tetracaine"]]],
["Systemic toxicity",["CNS: circumoral numbness, tinnitus, seizures, coma","CVS: bradycardia, hypotension, arrhythmias (bupivacaine)","Treatment: airway, O2, benzodiazepines, 20% lipid emulsion","Methaemoglobinaemia: prilocaine, benzocaine (methylene blue)"]]],
{i:"Local anaesthetics allow surgery and pain relief without general anaesthesia; their choice depends on onset, duration, toxicity and technique.",f:"LA (base) crosses membrane → ionised form inside\n → blocks voltage-gated Na⁺ channel (inner vestibule)\n → no action potential → conduction block\n Order: small C/Aδ (pain) → touch → motor; adrenaline prolongs",c:"Local anaesthetics block sodium channels to produce reversible regional anaesthesia; understanding classes, doses and toxicity — especially bupivacaine cardiotoxicity — ensures safe use."});

Z("pharm5",6,"ph",["Lignocaine (lidocaine) is the most widely used amide local anaesthetic; it acts fast, has intermediate duration, can be used by almost every route, and also works as an antiarrhythmic for ventricular arrhythmias.","The all-rounder: topical, injection, nerve block, spinal, and even IV to calm an irritable ventricle.",["Amide LA; onset fast (2–5 min), duration 1–2 h (with adrenaline 2–3 h); hepatic metabolism.","Preparations: 2% injection (± adrenaline 1:200,000), 5% heavy (spinal), 2–4% jelly/viscous, 10% spray, EMLA (lignocaine + prilocaine).","Uses: infiltration, nerve blocks, epidural, spinal, surface anaesthesia, IV regional; class IB antiarrhythmic (ventricular arrhythmias, post-MI, digitalis toxicity); neuropathic pain patches.","Max dose 4.5 mg/kg (7 mg/kg with adrenaline); toxicity — CNS (drowsiness, tinnitus, seizures), CVS depression; high first-pass — not effective orally."],"Lignocaine: 'Local + Lifeline for ventricular arrhythmias'."],
"Lignocaine (lidocaine) is an amide-linked local anaesthetic introduced in 1948; it is the most commonly used LA because of rapid onset, moderate duration, good tissue penetration and versatility. Properties: pKa 7.9, moderately lipid soluble and protein bound (~65%); onset 2–5 minutes; duration 1–2 hours (up to 3 h with adrenaline); vasodilator; metabolised in liver by CYP1A2/3A4 to active metabolites (monoethylglycinexylidide, glycinexylidide) — clearance reduced in liver disease, heart failure, with cimetidine and β-blockers; high hepatic first-pass, so ineffective orally. Preparations and uses: topical — 2% jelly (urethral catheterisation), 4% solution and 10% spray (airway, endoscopy), 5% ointment, viscous 2% (oral ulcers), EMLA cream (eutectic mixture with prilocaine for venepuncture in children); 5% patch for postherpetic neuralgia; injectable — 1–2% for infiltration, field blocks, nerve and plexus blocks (dental — with adrenaline), epidural (1–2%), 5% hyperbaric 'heavy' for spinal (now less used due to transient neurologic symptoms), 0.5% for intravenous regional anaesthesia (Bier block); systemic — class IB antiarrhythmic: blocks inactivated Na⁺ channels in depolarised ischaemic tissue, shortens action potential duration, suppresses automaticity; IV 1–1.5 mg/kg bolus then 1–4 mg/min infusion for ventricular tachycardia/fibrillation (especially in ischaemia, MI, digitalis toxicity), suppression of cough and pressor response during intubation, neuropathic and postoperative pain (IV infusion), status epilepticus (refractory, rarely). Maximum safe dose: 4.5 mg/kg plain (300 mg), 7 mg/kg with adrenaline (500 mg). Adverse effects: CNS — drowsiness, light-headedness, circumoral numbness, tinnitus, slurred speech, muscle twitching, confusion, convulsions (more prominent than cardiac effects), respiratory depression; CVS — hypotension, bradycardia, heart block, asystole at high levels; transient neurological symptoms after spinal; allergic reactions rare; methaemoglobinaemia (with EMLA in infants). Contraindications/cautions: hypersensitivity to amides, severe heart block, Adams–Stokes syndrome, severe hepatic impairment, porphyria; adrenaline-containing preparations avoided in end-arteries.",
[["Preparations and uses",[["Preparation","Use"],["2% injection ± adrenaline","Infiltration, nerve block, dental"],["1–2% epidural","Epidural anaesthesia/analgesia"],["5% heavy","Spinal anaesthesia"],["0.5% plain","IV regional (Bier block)"],["2% jelly, 4% solution, 10% spray","Surface anaesthesia"],["EMLA cream","Venepuncture in children"],["5% patch","Postherpetic neuralgia"],["IV bolus + infusion","Ventricular arrhythmias"]]],
["Kinetics",["Amide; hepatic metabolism (CYP1A2/3A4)","Onset 2–5 min; duration 1–2 h (3 h with adrenaline)","High first-pass — not oral","Max 4.5 mg/kg (7 mg/kg with adrenaline)"]]],
{c:"Lignocaine's speed, versatility and dual role as local anaesthetic and class IB antiarrhythmic make it indispensable, provided maximum doses are respected."});
